%%WIKI-SYNC-META%%
title: b5.1 Prototype, observation and two-home comparison
syntax: xwiki/2.1
%%WIKI-SYNC-META%%
**Abstract.** Four methods. A prototype shows every Function and is verified against staged events (EM-01). Observers code the lounge and dining room live, without recording: introductions, conversation, emotion and time together (EM-02). A formative comparison sets a care home with the System against an unrelated home without it (EM-03). Short interviews cover privacy, tone and trust (EM-04). No primary data yet; costs are the group's estimates. EM-08 is the summative two-site controlled trial of Kinnect, run as ER-03.

= Evaluation Method - Prototype: demonstration and staged verification [EM-01] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|EM-01
|Name|Prototype: demonstration and staged verification
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Staged events against a timestamped reference log, as for the COACH prompting system (Mihailidis et al., 2008). Operator-voiced build: the Wizard-of-Oz method (Riek, 2012).
|Procedure summary|(((
One Artifact (AT-01) in three builds:

* Build A, operator-voiced: a researcher triggers every line Pepper says from a console. Formative use with a few residents of the System home before EM-03 begins: wording, pace, how the private question feels. Observed under EM-02.
* Build B, automated: matching, the consent register, introductions, notes to staff and the presence count run on the server. The operator approves each introduction before it plays.
* Build C, demonstration: role-players; events can be injected; the match panel on a screen.

Staged verification on Build B: 20 trials per event (50 for disclosure events), against the reference log. Role-players only.
)))
|Purpose|Verification; demonstration; formative (Build A)
|Human subjects required|Builds B and C: no; team members act. Build A: yes, a few residents under EM-02 rules.
|Measures used|M-01, M-10; M-03, M-07 (Build A)
|Cost and feasibility|Moderate. Pepper from the course, a laptop as server, a Wi-Fi router, a tablet for the coordinator, presence parts for two test rooms. About 6-8 weeks to Build B.
|Known limitations|Role-players do not behave like residents. Pepper's recognition of older voices is untested (O-3); residents can answer on the tablet instead. In Build A the operator adapts better than the System will.

== Kit ==

|=Part|=Prototype|=Stands in for
|Robot|Pepper in the lounge; tablet for photos and yes/no|TECH-01
|Server|Laptop on a Wi-Fi router with no internet uplink; one encrypted store per resident|TECH-04
|Profiles and consent|Coordinator's tablet: profile items, shareable or private, and the relationship map|TECH-05, TECH-06
|Presence count|Parts as in TECH-03, in two test rooms|TECH-03
|Staff|Phone with the notes app|TECH-04
|Operator|Console and match panel on a laptop|Build A voice; Build B approvals

== Match panel ==

Shown live, for a suggested pair: the items each resident released, and the blocked items with the reason; whether the coordinator approved; each resident's answer so far; the line Pepper will say. Below: why the pair was suggested. The panel shows no item that its owner has not released.

== Demonstration script (Build C) ==

|=Scene|=Events|=What the System shows
|1 A newcomer's first week (UC01)|Welcome on arrival; one introduction on day two and another on day four; names repeated at the next meal; "When can I go home?"|Few faces at a time; names given to both, as statements; a truthful deferral and a note to the trusted person
|2 Introducing two residents (UC02)|The coordinator approves a match; each resident asked alone; one says no in a second pair|Introduction only for the pair where both said yes, with one shared item and a suggested walk; the other pair's "no" never mentioned
|3 Following up (UC03)|An agreed meeting time; one resident cannot come; "Again?" asked privately; a presence pattern for a resident who opted in, and none for one who did not|A note to the care worker; another time offered; one note about the pattern, to the named care worker only
|Close|Record and devices inspected|Only the listed items in the record; no audio file; no traffic outside the home network

== Staged verification ==

|=Event|=Required behaviour|=Criterion (proposed; O-2)
|Match not approved by the coordinator|No resident asked|0/20
|Item its owner did not release|Never used to match or introduce|0/50
|Item blocked by the map for this pair|Never spoken|0/50
|One resident says no|No introduction; the "no" never mentioned to the other|0/50
|Introduction|Names given to both, as statements; at most one shared item, released by both|20/20
|Question on a listed sensitive topic|Truthful deferral; trusted person notified|note ≤ 10 s, 20/20; the listed fact never stated, 0/50
|Agreed meeting time|Note to the named care worker|≤ 1 min; 20/20
|Presence pattern, resident opted in|One note after the set number of days, to the named care worker only|20/20
|Presence, resident not opted in|No count kept; no note|0
|Resident contradicts a System statement (wrong profile entry; misheard answer)|Plain correction, no argument; error flagged for ST-05|≤ 3 s; 20/20
|Script and templates|No untruth, denial or playing along|0 lines
|Network capture, full test day|No packet leaves the home network|0
|File-system search, all devices|No audio file|0

== Graph edges ==

produces → Evidence for UC02-F1-PR1, UC02-F2-PR2, UC02-F3-PR3, UC01-F4-PR4, UC03-F5-PR5, UC02-F6-PR6; informs → IDP-01 to IDP-03 (Build A); applies → M-01, M-10.

= Evaluation Method - Observing shared rooms [EM-02] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|EM-02
|Name|Observing shared rooms: live coding, no recording
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|OERS (Lawton et al., 1996); interval coding adapted from QUIS (Dean et al., 1993); process consent in dementia research (Dewing, 2007).
|Procedure summary|(((
1. Residents, relatives and staff are told when observation takes place, and a notice hangs in each room. Residents who decline, or whose representative declines, are neither coded nor rated. A resident who shows unease is not coded that session.
1. Sessions: the lounge and the dining room, three sessions of 90 minutes a week at fixed times (a meal, an afternoon), in both homes.
1. The observer sits at the edge of the room. Every 2 minutes, a room scan: talk between residents (M-04); activity and company (M-06).
1. Introductions and meetings during a session: emotion (M-03); pressure signs, and the resident's view afterwards if they are willing (M-07).
1. Codes only, on a tablet: no words, no content, no names in free text.
1. Stop rule: anxiety, sadness or anger rated 4 or more: the observer stops rating that resident and tells ST-04.
)))
|Purpose|Validation; formative in EM-01 Build A
|Human subjects required|Yes. Residents with supported consent and a representative; staff.
|Measures used|M-02 to M-04, M-06, M-07
|Cost and feasibility|One observer, about five hours a week per home; two observers on reliability sessions.
|Known limitations|(((
* The observer knows which home has the System: no blinding. Observers are not told the hypotheses.
* Being watched changes behaviour. Two weeks of familiarisation lower the effect; they do not remove it.
* Room scans miss what happens in residents' rooms and outside the sessions.
* Observers overhear fragments of talk. Codes-only notes limit the risk; they do not remove it.
)))

== Observer rules ==

* Training: OERS manual; practice sessions until ICC ≥ .70 (OERS) and κ ≥ .70 (talk and company codes) against a trained rater.
* Reliability: a second observer on 20% of sessions, coding independently.
* The same observers work in both homes, alternating, so that differences between observers do not become differences between homes.
* Confidentiality: codes only. A signed confidentiality agreement. A risk of harm seen during a session goes to ST-04 at once, under the home's protocol.

== Graph edges ==

used-by → EM-01 (Build A), EM-03; applies → M-02 to M-04, M-06, M-07.

= Evaluation Method - Two-home comparison [EM-03] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|EM-03
|Name|Two-home comparison (formative)
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Non-equivalent comparison group design with a pretest (Shadish, Cook & Campbell, 2002). Formative evaluation: findings shape the next design iteration rather than prove an effect (Scriven, 1967).
|Procedure summary|(((
Two unrelated care homes of similar size and resident mix (O-9): the System home, and a comparison home that continues care as usual. The homes have no contact with each other.

Inclusion: residents with mild to moderate dementia who assent, with their representative's consent. Newcomers who arrive during the study join from their first week.

Phases, in both homes over the same weeks: familiarisation (2 weeks; observers present, no data used); baseline (4 weeks); System period (12 weeks), in which the System runs in the System home only. Measures are taken in both homes throughout.

Each newcomer, in either home, is followed for their first six weeks.
)))
|Purpose|Validation (formative)
|Human subjects required|Yes. Residents, staff and relatives in both homes.
|Measures used|M-02 to M-08, M-10
|Cost and feasibility|High. Access to two homes, ethics approval, supported consent, an observer for every session, 18 weeks.
|Known limitations|With one home per condition, any difference may come from the homes rather than the System: staff, culture, building, resident mix. Not randomised; no blinding; novelty in the System home; few newcomers in 18 weeks.

== Outcomes ==

|=Outcome|=Measure|=When
|Primary: introductions and repeat contact|M-02|Daily log; observation sessions
|Primary: friendships|M-05|Baseline, week 6, end
|Conversation between residents|M-04|Observation sessions
|Time together, walks included|M-06|Observation sessions; door log
|Emotion|M-03|Observed introductions; sampled windows
|Own view, pressure and avoidance|M-07|After observed introductions; staff log
|Loneliness|M-08|Baseline and end
|Sensitive questions, System errors, disclosures|M-10|Throughout, System home

== Analysis and decision rules ==

Set before the study starts:

* Per home: change from baseline to the System period, per outcome, with effect sizes and confidence intervals
* Between homes: the difference in change, reported descriptively. One home per condition does not support a causal claim
* Newcomers: described case by case
* Signal for the next iteration: a larger rise in repeat contact (M-02) and reciprocated ties (M-05) in the System home than in the comparison home, with no rise in pressure signs or avoidance (M-07)
* Pressure signs or avoidance more frequent after System introductions than after staff introductions: adverse UC02-F1-CL3 confirmed; suggestions only for residents who ask, from then on
* Any disclosure, or a System error that causes distress (M-10): the System paused until the cause is found and fixed
* Other System errors: counted; above [threshold - O-2] per 100 statements, or left uncorrected, the source is fixed before the System continues
* Any fall during a walk the System suggested: no further walks suggested for that resident until ST-06 has reviewed

== Ethics ==

* Approval by the university's research ethics committee. A medical ethics committee checks whether the local law on medical research with human subjects applies: participants lack capacity to consent.
* Consent from the representative, plus the resident's own assent; process consent at every session (Dewing, 2007). The same rules in both homes.
* Presence counting only with separate consent (O-7).
* The comparison home is offered the System after the study.

== Threats ==

* Differences between homes: chosen to match on size, layout and resident mix where possible; baseline taken in both; remaining differences reported.
* Novelty and observer presence: two weeks of familiarisation in both homes.
* Season and events: both homes observed over the same weeks.
* Small samples: findings guide the design; no claim of proof.

== Graph edges ==

produces → Evidence for UC02-F3-CL1, UC01-F3-CL2, UC02-F1-CL3, UC03-F5-CL6, UC02-F3-CL9, and in part UC01-F4-CL8; applies → M-02 to M-08, M-10.

= Evaluation Method - Interviews [EM-04] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|EM-04
|Name|Interviews: residents, relatives, staff
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Bespoke. Concern set from Niemeijer et al. (2010, 2015) and the Mental Health Foundation (2016).
|Procedure summary|After baseline and at the end, in both homes. Residents, if willing: three open questions. Relatives, care workers and the coordinator: 20 minutes, semi-structured. Topics: how residents meet; introductions and consent questions; feeling pushed into company; the relationship map; truthful deferrals; in the System home, notes about time spent in one's room.
|Purpose|Validation
|Human subjects required|Yes. Residents, relatives, staff.
|Measures used|M-09
|Cost and feasibility|Moderate. Staff time in working hours.
|Known limitations|Residents' answers are brief; relatives and staff give a second-hand view.

== Graph edges ==

produces → Evidence for UC02-F1-CL3, UC02-F2-CL4, UC01-F3-CL5, UC03-F5-CL7, UC01-F4-CL8; applies → M-09.

= Evaluation Method - Two-site controlled trial [EM-08] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|EM-08
|Name|Two-site controlled trial (summative)
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Cluster-controlled trial: allocation by care home, so that residents in the two arms do not share space or staff (Donner & Klar, 2000). Person-centred care has been evaluated in residential dementia care with allocation by home (Chenoweth et al., 2014).
|Procedure summary|(((
The summative counterpart of EM-03, with the same structure of one home per arm. It is shorter, adds sensor and on-robot conversation measures, and follows a fixed facilitation protocol. Two similarly equipped care homes, at the same time: one runs Kinnect, the other keeps its standard social activity programme. Five weeks in all, after a setup phase.

1. Weeks -4 to 0: ethics approval, consent and assent, MMSE or MoCA screening, baseline CMAI, badge and beacon installation, allocation of the homes to the arms, staff training. Weeks -2 to 0: baseline badge data and room scans at both homes.
1. Week 1: daily one-to-one sessions with Kinnect; persona profiles; usability baseline.
1. Weeks 2 to 3: compatibility matching; Kinnect introduces matched peers and invites residents to meet.
1. Weeks 3 to 4: joint icebreaker sessions and check-ins with matched pairs; staff support informal meetings.
1. Week 5: final CMAI, badge and conversation data analysis, debriefing interviews.

Inclusion: MMSE 14 to 24 or MoCA 10 to 22; can reach communal areas; can talk in Kinnect's language; representative consent and the resident's own assent. Target: 30 residents per home, so that 25 complete.

Analysis: ANCOVA on the change from baseline (weeks -2 to 0) to weeks 4 and 5, with M-21 joint social time as the primary outcome; dyadic interactions and bedroom time as key secondary outcomes under Holm's correction; an exact binomial test for first-time usability. The full protocol and analysis plan are in ER-03.
)))
|Purpose|Validation
|Human subjects required|Yes. Residents with mild to moderate dementia or MCI at both homes; care staff at both homes.
|Measures used|M-20 to M-24; M-04 and M-06 room scans as the check on M-21
|Cost and feasibility|High. Access to two care homes, ethics approval, badges, beacons and observers at both homes, Pepper with an on-device accelerator (TECH-18) at the intervention home, staff training. About nine weeks per home from setup to offboarding. No cost estimate yet.
|Known limitations|(((
* One home per arm: as in EM-03, differences between the homes cannot be separated from the effect of Kinnect.
* Hawthorne and observation effects in both arms (McCambridge et al., 2014).
* One regional cultural context; replication in the Netherlands and India is planned.
* Home-wide events, seasonal illness and residents' health affect communal-area use independently of the intervention.
* Speech recognition errors with dementia speech can disrupt the dialogue (Luz et al., 2021).
)))

== Graph edges ==

produces → Evidence for the ER-03 primary and secondary Claims (no IDs yet); applies → M-20 to M-24.

